course: dictionary
lesson: 5852
title: One word, two meanings: mall
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mall is a large building with lots of shops, restaurants and sometimes a cinema. People often say shopping mall. In the USA, Canada and Australia, families say they are going «to the mall» to shop and meet friends. In the UK and Ireland, people usually say shopping centre instead.
explain: In the UK, The Mall is also a long, wide street or path with trees, in London. The words around the word tell you which meaning it has. If the sentence talks about shops, a food court or escalators, it means the building. If it talks about walking along a wide path, it means the street.
explain: Mall is also a safety word. «If you get lost in a shop or mall, you stay where you are or go to a till or checkout and tell a staff member, who can help find your family.» The clues are stay, till and staff member, so the sentence is about getting help, not about walking along a street.
fact: Mall is a noun. One is a mall, and more than one is malls.
fact: «Mall» can mean a large building with many shops, restaurants and sometimes a cinema, often with a car park.
fact: «The Mall» in London is a long, wide street or path for walking, with trees.
fact: If you get separated in a mall, agree on a meeting point before you go in, and ask a shop worker or security guard with a name badge for help.
fact: A girl in Florida who got lost stayed inside the shop, went to a counter and told the worker her mum's phone number, which she knew by heart.
exercise: «On escalators in malls you hold the rail and never run up the down escalator.» In this sentence, what is a mall? | A kind of bus stop | A type of garden | *A large building with shops and escalators | A long, wide street with trees
exercise: The Mall in London is a famous place for walking. What kind of place is The Mall? | A school for young children | A shop full of toys | *A long, wide street or path | A cinema with many screens
exercise: «If you get lost in a shop or mall, you stay where you are.» What should you do if you get lost? | Run around until you find your family | Hide behind a rack of clothes | Leave the building and walk home alone | *Stay where you are and tell a staff member
test: «A five-year-old in a big mall in Ohio wandered off to look at toys.» Which word tells you he went away without telling anyone? | Toys | *Wandered | Ohio | Five-year-old
test: What is the best thing to do if you are separated from your family in a mall? | Walk out into the car park alone | Hide until they find you | Ask any stranger you pass | *Agree a meeting point, or ask a shop worker with a name badge
exam: Which sentence uses mall to mean a long, wide street? | Hold the rail on the escalator at the mall | *The Mall in London is a long, wide path lined with trees | We met at the mall to buy new trainers | The escalators in the mall were broken
